<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for Preceipts.app's main executable.

  This file is deliberately empty, and both absences are decisions.

  No com.apple.security.app-sandbox. The app ships a daemon that supervises
  arbitrary user processes in arbitrary worktrees and binds listening sockets;
  the sandbox forbids both, and there is no temporary exception that grants
  "run whatever the developer's project runs". A sandboxed build would be a
  build that cannot boot an environment, which is the product. That rules out
  the Mac App Store and makes Developer ID plus notarization the only
  distribution path.

  No keychain-access-groups either — and this one we learned by trying it.
  Signing the bundle with that key produced a binary the kernel SIGKILLs at
  the first page fault: `CODESIGNING / Invalid Page`, with an empty
  codeSigningTeamID on the dead process. It is a *restricted* entitlement, so
  outside the App Store it is authorised by an embedded provisioning profile,
  not by a certificate. Two further facts make the whole idea wrong rather
  than merely blocked: the key governs the data-protection keychain, while our
  secrets live in the file-based one; and `preceipts` is installed standalone
  as a bare Mach-O, which cannot carry a provisioning profile at all, so the
  entitlement would kill the CLI on every non-bundle install.

  Sharing is done instead by the ACL on each keychain item, which names a code
  requirement — the same Team ID, and nothing else on the machine. See
  secrets.rs. That needs no entitlement, works for the standalone CLI, and is
  the thing the open-ACL workaround was standing in for.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
